# Hoare logic for a small imperative language
#
# {p} c {q}: if c runs from a state satisfying p and terminates, q holds after
# (partial correctness). Assertions are formulas of the library (EQ, ∧, ¬, ...)
# and conditions of IF and WHILE are formulas too, so the rules can put them
# into assertions directly.
#
# Assignment, {Q[e/x]} x = e; {Q}: Q[e/x] can't be computed by string
# substitution on a value, but fol.btp's judgements compute it: ABS turns the
# parameter x into V(0), OPEN puts the closed term e in its place. So rAssign
# needs program variables to be parameters (PAR, ordered by PLT after Z; see
# ../gcd.btp). Programs with constants for variables instead state their own
# assignment axioms for the assertion shapes they use (see ../hoare.btp).

<?py load('fol.btp') ?>
<?py load('strings.btp') ?>
## Program syntax: assignment, skip, sequence (x y, from strings.btp), IF, WHILE
rTmAsg   : x = y;
rTmSkip  : ;
rTmIf    : IF (b) { c } ELSE { d }
rTmWhile : WHILE (b) { c }

## Rules
rSkip     : {p} ; {p}
rAssign   : ABS(0,x,q,f) -> OPEN(0,f,e,p) -> {p} x = e; {q}
rSeq      : {p} a {q} -> {q} b {r} -> {p} a b {r}
rIf       : {(p∧b)} c {q} -> {(p∧¬b)} d {q} -> {p} IF (b) { c } ELSE { d } {q}
rWhile    : {(p∧b)} c {p} -> {p} WHILE (b) { c } {(p∧¬b)}
rConsPre  : ⊢(p=>q) -> {q} c {r} -> {p} c {r}
rConsPost : {p} c {q} -> ⊢(q=>r) -> {p} c {r}
